  <h3>2000s — The Super-Cycle and the 2008 Crash</h3>
  <p>The 2000s witnessed crude oil's most dramatic cycle in history. Prices rose from ~$25 in early 2000 to an all-time high of $132.83 in July 2008, driven by surging Chinese demand, constrained supply, and financial speculation. The Global Financial Crisis then triggered a collapse to $41.34 by December 2008 — a 69% peak-to-trough decline in five months. This decade exemplifies the extreme boom-bust dynamics of crude oil.</p>

