    </div>
  </div>

  <p>This report examines the full trajectory of global crude oil prices from January 1970 to March 2026, covering {n} monthly data points. Over this {years:.0f}-year span, crude oil has transitioned from a low, stable-priced commodity (starting at {fmt_price(first_price)}) through multiple price regimes — the oil shocks of the 1970s, the collapse of the mid-1980s, the super-cycle of the 2000s, the 2020 pandemic demand shock, and the post-2022 geopolitical repricing. The compounded annual growth rate of <strong>{cagr * 100:.2f}%</strong> reflects this long-term ascent, though the path has been anything but smooth: the standard deviation of monthly prices ({fmt_price(std_dev)}) is {std_dev / mean_price * 100:.0f}% of the mean, underscoring extreme episodic volatility.</p>

  <p>The single largest month-over-month price shock was a <strong class="neg">{max_mom_pct_decrease['pct']:.1f}%</strong> decline in {fmt_date(max_mom_pct_decrease['date'])}. The largest monthly increase was <strong class="pos">{max_mom_pct_increase['pct']:.1f}%</strong> in {fmt_date(max_mom_pct_increase['date'])}. These extremes illustrate the asymmetric risk profile of crude oil as an asset class.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 2: LONG-TERM PRICE TRAJECTORY ───────────────────────────── -->
<section id="long-term-trend">
  <h2>2. Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
  <p>The full monthly time series reveals distinct price regimes driven by geopolitics, supply dynamics, and macroeconomic cycles. Key inflection points include the 1973–74 oil embargo, the 1979 Iranian Revolution, the 1986 price collapse, the 1990 Gulf War spike, the 2008 super-spike and subsequent crash, the 2014–15 shale-driven correction, the 2020 COVID-19 demand shock, and the 2022 Russia-Ukraine price surge.</p>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>Monthly Crude Oil Price (USD/bbl) — Full Time Series</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="fullSeriesChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>Annual Average Crude Oil Price</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="yearlyAvgChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <p>The all-time nominal peak was recorded in <strong>{fmt_date(dates[max_idx])}</strong> at <strong>{fmt_price(max_price)}</strong>. The all-time low was <strong>{fmt_price(min_price)}</strong> in <strong>{fmt_date(dates[min_idx])}</strong>. The price range of <strong>{fmt_price(price_range)}</strong> represents a {price_range / min_price:.0f}x multiple between the minimum and maximum monthly observations.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 3: DECADE-BY-DECADE ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="decade-by-decade">
  <h2>3. Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p>Decomposing the data by decade reveals the structural evolution of the global crude oil market — from the relatively tranquil pre-1973 era to the high-volatility environment of the 2000s onward.</p>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>Average Monthly Price by Decade (with Standard Deviation)</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="decadeBarChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>Price Distribution by Decade (Box Plot)</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="decadeBoxPlot"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Months</th><th>Avg |MoM%|</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''
for d in decade_order:
    ds = decade_stats[d]
    vol = decade_volatility.get(d, 0)
    html += f'      <tr><td class="highlight">{decade_label(d)}</td><td>{fmt_price(ds["avg"])}</td><td>{fmt_price(ds["min"])}</td><td>{fmt_price(ds["max"])}</td><td>{fmt_price(ds["std"])}</td><td>{ds["count"]}</td><td>{vol:.2f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>1970s — The Oil Shock Decade</h3>
  <p>The 1970s saw crude oil transition from a stable, low-cost commodity ($1.21–$2.70 in 1970–1973) to a geopolitically weaponized asset. The 1973 Arab Oil Embargo drove prices from $4.10 in October 1973 to $13.00 by January 1974 — a threefold increase in three months. The 1979 Iranian Revolution triggered a second shock, pushing prices from ~$14.50 in December 1978 to $40.75 by November 1979 — nearly tripling in under a year. By the end of the decade, crude had established itself as a volatile, strategically critical commodity.</p>

  <h3>1980s — Boom, Bust, and the 1986 Collapse</h3>
  <p>The early 1980s saw elevated but gradually declining prices as conservation efforts and new non-OPEC supply (North Sea, Alaska) came online. The defining event was the 1986 price collapse: from $24.68 in January 1986 to $9.62 by July 1986 — a 61% decline driven by Saudi Arabia's decision to abandon the swing producer role and flood the market. Prices recovered modestly through the late 1980s but remained in a $12–$20 range.</p>

  <h3>1990s — Relative Stability and the Asian Financial Crisis</h3>
  <p>The 1990s opened with the Gulf War price spike ($34.50 in October 1990) followed by a rapid normalization. For most of the decade, prices traded in a $15–$25 range — a period of relative calm. The Asian Financial Crisis of 1997–98 drove prices to a decade low of $10.41 in December 1998, demonstrating crude's sensitivity to global demand shocks even absent supply disruptions.</p>

  <h3>2000s — The Super-Cycle and the 2008 Crash</h3>
  <p>The 2000s witnessed crude oil's most dramatic cycle in history. Prices rose from ~$25 in early 2000 to an all-time high of $132.83 in July 2008, driven by surging Chinese demand, constrained supply, and financial speculation. The Global Financial Crisis then triggered a collapse to $41.34 by December 2008 — a 69% peak-to-trough decline in five months. This decade exemplifies the extreme boom-bust dynamics of crude oil.</p>

  <h3>2010s — Shale Revolution and the 2014 Price War</h3>
  <p>Prices recovered to $90–$115 range in 2011–2014, supported by tight OPEC supply and geopolitical risk premiums (Arab Spring, Iran sanctions). The US shale revolution fundamentally altered the supply landscape. OPEC's November 2014 decision to defend market share rather than price triggered a second collapse: from $108.37 in June 2014 to $36.57 in December 2015 — a 66% decline. Prices stabilized in the $45–$70 range for the remainder of the decade.</p>

  <h3>2020s — Pandemic Shock, Geopolitical Repricing, and Projections</h3>
  <p>The 2020s began with the COVID-19 pandemic driving prices to $21.04 in April 2020 — the lowest since 2002 — as global demand evaporated. An unprecedented recovery followed, with prices reaching $116.80 by June 2022 following Russia's invasion of Ukraine. The data extends through early 2026, capturing the moderation from wartime highs into the $60–$88 range, reflecting both the rebalancing of global energy flows and forward-looking estimates.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 4: VOLATILITY & SHOCKS ──────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="volatility-and-shocks">
  <h2>4. Price Volatility &amp; Major Shocks</h2>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>12-Month Rolling Standard Deviation of Price</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="rollingVolChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>Month-over-Month Price Change (Absolute, USD/bbl)</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="momChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Largest Monthly Price Increases</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Absolute Change</th><th>% Change</th><th>Price Before</th><th>Price After</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
'''
for mc in top_mom_increases:
    idx_prev = next(i for i, m in enumerate(mom_changes) if m['date'] == mc['date']) - 1
    before = prices[idx_prev + 1 - 1]  # mom_changes[i] corresponds to prices[i+1]
    # Let's fix: mom_changes has data starting from index 1 of prices. mom_changes[0] = prices[1] - prices[0]
    # Find the correct before price
    mc_idx = next(i for i, r in enumerate(rows) if r['date'] == mc['date'])
    before_p = prices[mc_idx - 1]
    after_p = prices[mc_idx]
    html += f'      <tr><td>{fmt_date(mc["date"])}</td><td class="pos">+{fmt_price(mc["abs"])}</td><td class="pos">+{mc["pct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{fmt_price(before_p)}</td><td>{fmt_price(after_p)}</td></tr>\n'

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Top 10 Largest Monthly Price Decreases</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Absolute Change</th><th>% Change</th><th>Price Before</th><th>Price After</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
'''
for mc in top_mom_decreases:
    mc_idx = next(i for i, r in enumerate(rows) if r['date'] == mc['date'])
    before_p = prices[mc_idx - 1]
    after_p = prices[mc_idx]
    html += f'      <tr><td>{fmt_date(mc["date"])}</td><td class="neg">{fmt_price(mc["abs"])}</td><td class="neg">{mc["pct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{fmt_price(before_p)}</td><td>{fmt_price(after_p)}</td></tr>\n'

# YoY extremes
max_yoy_pct_inc = max(yoy_changes, key=lambda x: x['pct'])
max_yoy_pct_dec = min(yoy_changes, key=lambda x: x['pct'])

html += f'''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Major Year-over-Year Moves</h3>
  <p>The largest year-over-year price increase occurred in <strong>{fmt_date(max_yoy_pct_inc['date'])}</strong> at <strong class="pos">+{max_yoy_pct_inc['pct']:.1f}%</strong>, while the largest YoY decline occurred in <strong>{fmt_date(max_yoy_pct_dec['date'])}</strong> at <strong class="neg">{max_yoy_pct_dec['pct']:.1f}%</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Historical Shock Events Correlated with Extreme Moves</h3>
  <ul class="bullet-list">
    <li><strong>1973 Arab Oil Embargo (Oct 1973):</strong> Prices surged from $2.70 to $4.10 (+51.9%) in a single month, then to $13.00 by January 1974.</li>
    <li><strong>1979 Iranian Revolution (May 1979):</strong> Monthly price jumped 49.4% from $22.43 to $33.50, part of a 185% increase from Dec 1978 to Nov 1979.</li>
    <li><strong>1986 Saudi Price War (Feb–Jul 1986):</strong> Prices collapsed 61% from $24.68 to $9.62 in six months, with the largest single-month drop of 35.5% in February 1986.</li>
    <li><strong>1990 Gulf War (Aug–Oct 1990):</strong> Iraq's invasion of Kuwait drove prices from $17.17 to $34.50 in three months (+100.9%), followed by an equally rapid normalization.</li>
    <li><strong>2008 Global Financial Crisis (Jul–Dec 2008):</strong> From the all-time high of $132.83 in July to $41.34 in December — a 69% collapse, including a 36.5% monthly drop in October 2008.</li>
    <li><strong>2014–2015 OPEC Price War (Jun 2014–Dec 2015):</strong> Prices declined 66% from $108.37 to $36.57 over 18 months as US shale supply surged and OPEC defended market share.</li>
    <li><strong>2020 COVID-19 Pandemic (Mar–Apr 2020):</strong> Prices fell 34.7% in a single month (March to April 2020) as global lockdowns crushed demand, reaching $21.04.</li>
    <li><strong>2022 Russia-Ukraine Conflict (Feb–Mar 2022):</strong> Prices surged 20.2% in one month from $93.54 to $112.40, peaking at $116.80 in June 2022.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 5: STATISTICAL SUMMARY ──────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="statistical-summary">
  <h2>5. Statistical Summary &amp; Distribution</h2>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Descriptive Statistics</h3>
      <table>
        <tr><td>Count</td><td class="highlight">{n}</td></tr>
