    <div>
      <h3>Distribution Characteristics</h3>
      <p>The price distribution is <strong>positively skewed</strong> — the mean ({fmt_price(mean_price)}) exceeds the median ({fmt_price(median_price)}), indicating that extreme high-price episodes pull the average upward. This is characteristic of commodity prices, where supply shocks produce sharp upward spikes followed by gradual mean reversion.</p>
      <p>The interquartile range (IQR) is <strong>{fmt_price(p75)} – {fmt_price(p25)} = {fmt_price(p75 - p25)}</strong>, containing the middle 50% of monthly observations. The wide gap between the 10th percentile ({fmt_price(p10)}) and the 90th percentile ({fmt_price(p90)}) — a {p90 - p10:.0f}x multiple — underscores the extraordinary range of pricing environments crude oil has traversed.</p>
      <p>The coefficient of variation (CV = σ/μ) is <strong>{std_dev / mean_price:.2f}</strong>, indicating high relative dispersion — crude oil prices are more volatile relative to their mean than most other major commodity classes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-card">
    <h4>Price Distribution Histogram (Monthly Observations)</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="histogramChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 6: OUTLOOK & CONCLUSIONS ────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
  <h2>6. Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>

  <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
  <ul class="bullet-list">
    <li><strong>Structural price escalation:</strong> Crude oil prices have grown at a CAGR of {cagr * 100:.2f}% over {years:.0f} years, but this masks extreme cyclicality. The price has exhibited multiple boom-bust cycles with peak-to-trough drawdowns of 60–70%.</li>
    <li><strong>Increasing absolute volatility:</strong> Rolling 12-month price volatility (standard deviation) has risen dramatically in absolute terms since 2000, reflecting larger price levels and more frequent extreme events. The 2008 and 2020 periods represent the two highest volatility regimes.</li>
    <li><strong>The 2010s as the highest average decade:</strong> With a mean monthly price of {fmt_price(decade_stats[highest_avg_decade]['avg'])}, the {decade_label(highest_avg_decade)} recorded the highest average crude oil prices, driven by the post-financial-crisis recovery, tight OPEC policy, and geopolitical risk premiums — followed by the shale-induced correction.</li>
    <li><strong>Geopolitics remain the dominant driver:</strong> Every major price spike in the dataset (1973, 1979, 1990, 2008, 2022) has a clear geopolitical or macroeconomic crisis origin. Supply-side shocks tend to produce sharper and more sustained price increases than demand-side shocks.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent trend (2020s):</strong> Following the pandemic trough of $21.04 in April 2020, prices recovered to $116.80 by June 2022 — a 455% increase in 26 months. As of the latest data ({fmt_date(last_date)}), crude oil trades at {fmt_price(last_price)}, reflecting the rebalancing of global supply chains away from Russian energy and moderating demand growth expectations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Concluding Observations</h3>
  <p>The trajectory of global crude oil prices from 1970 to 2026 illustrates the commodity's evolution from a stable, low-cost input to a highly financialized and geopolitically sensitive asset. The data confirms that crude oil does not follow a simple mean-reverting or trending process — instead, it is characterized by long periods of relative stability punctuated by sharp, event-driven re-pricings.</p>
  <p>For analysts, the key implication is that crude oil price forecasting requires scenario-based frameworks that account for supply disruption risk, OPEC+ policy shifts, technological change (shale, energy transition), and macroeconomic demand cycles. Historical volatility patterns suggest that extended periods of low volatility have historically preceded major price regime changes — a pattern worth monitoring in the current environment.</p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.85rem;"><em>Note: Data from 2025–2026 may include forward-looking estimates or projections. All statistics are computed from nominal USD prices with no inflation adjustment. Source: Kaggle — Global Fuel Price Trends 1970–2026 (minahilfatima12328).</em></p>
</section>

